      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\), we first need to determine the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) by translating the endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\) by 2 units to the right and 3 units up.\n\nThe original endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\) are \\((4,1)\\) and \\((-8,5)\\). Translating these points by 2 units to the right and 3 units up:\n\n1. For the point \\((4,1)\\):\n   - Moving to the right by 2 units: \\( 4 + 2 = 6 \\)\n   - Moving up by 3 units: \\( 1 + 3 = 4 \\)\n   - So, the new endpoint is \\((6,4)\\).\n\n2. For the point \\((-8,5)\\):\n   - Moving to the right by 2 units: \\( -8 + 2 = -6 \\)\n   - Moving up by 3 units: \\( 5 + 3 = 8 \\)\n   - So, the new endpoint is \\((-6,8)\\).\n\nNext, we calculate the midpoint of the translated segment \\( s_2 \\) using the midpoint formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Midpoint} = \\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the translated endpoints \\((6,4)\\) and \\((-6,8)\\):\n\\[\n\\text{Midpoint} = \\left( \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Midpoint} = \\left( \\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Midpoint} = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nThus, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 6)}\n\\]",
